WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.835
Hello everyone, and welcome back. We
are QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:09.147
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:13.616
<c.asks>And today, we want a much shorter
way to write down a quantum code.</c>

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:18.789
In lecture seven we described
the bit flip code in two

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:21.495
quite different ways, without
making any fuss about it.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:25.776
The first way was to write out its
states, so here are all eight basis

00:00:25.776 --> 00:00:29.969
states of three qubits, and you can see
that the code only ever uses these two.

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:35.457
The second way was a pair of questions,
do the first two bits agree, and do the

00:00:35.457 --> 00:00:39.845
last two agree, which we wrote as Z Z
on one pair and Z Z on the other.

00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:44.539
Now watch what happens to each of these
descriptions as I keep adding qubits.

00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:47.645
<c.asks>The left one explodes.
But couldn't you just</c>

00:00:47.645 --> 00:00:49.949
<c.asks>store the amplitudes
that aren't zero?</c>

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:54.544
Storing only the ones that aren't
zero is a good instinct, but for the

00:00:54.544 --> 00:00:58.055
codes at the end of this lecture,
even those keep doubling as the code

00:00:58.056 --> 00:01:01.262
grows, while the list on the right
just gets a few lines longer.

00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:04.868
So in this section, I want to
show you how a short list of

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:07.637
questions like this one can
pin down a quantum state.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:13.178
Let's start with the alphabet these
questions are written in, which has

00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:17.246
only four letters, the identity and
the three Pauli matrices X, Y and Z.

00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:22.380
To act on several qubits you just line the
letters up, one for each qubit, so this

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.646
string means X on the first qubit, nothing
on the second, and Z on the third.

00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:30.142
We also allow a sign out in
front, and all the strings you

00:01:30.142 --> 00:01:32.846
can make this way are together
called the Pauli group.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:37.668
<c.asks>You called them questions, though. How
is a string of matrices a question?</c>

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.860
It's a question because
each string we'll use has

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.678
only two possible answers,
plus one and minus one.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.905
Take Z Z on the first two
qubits, and feed it this state,

00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:51.933
where the first two bits agree.
Each Z turns a one into a minus

00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:55.441
sign, the two signs cancel, and
the answer is plus one.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:59.610
But here the first two bits
disagree, so only one minus sign

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.006
appears, nothing cancels it, and
the answer is minus one.

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:08.297
Now, once you have two questions, you
need to know whether one state can

00:02:08.297 --> 00:02:12.485
answer plus one to both, and that comes
down to whether the two strings commute.

00:02:13.067 --> 00:02:17.620
For a single qubit you already know how
this goes, because X followed by Z is

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.293
minus Z followed by X, and that's what
we mean when we say the two anticommute.

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:26.313
For longer strings you compare
them slot by slot, and every slot

00:02:26.313 --> 00:02:29.650
where the two hold different
letters, with neither of them the

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.166
identity, contributes one of
these minus signs.

00:02:32.733 --> 00:02:36.513
So here's one for you to try.
This is X X on two qubits,

00:02:36.513 --> 00:02:39.811
and this is Z Z on the same
two. Do they commute?

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.437
<c.asks>X and Z don't commute,
so I'd say no.</c>

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:48.230
You're right that X and Z don't
commute, but notice that there

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.693
are two slots here, and each of
them gives a minus sign. Those

00:02:51.694 --> 00:02:54.987
two minus signs cancel, so these
strings commute after all.

00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:59.408
And that's the whole rule. An
even number of clashing slots

00:02:59.408 --> 00:03:02.864
means the two strings commute,
while an odd number means they

00:03:02.864 --> 00:03:06.608
anticommute, and then no state at
all can answer plus one to both.

00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:11.241
So let's take a list of strings
that do commute, and collect

00:03:11.241 --> 00:03:13.770
every state that answers plus
one to all of them.

00:03:14.367 --> 00:03:18.225
With two qubits there are four
basis states, and the question Z

00:03:18.225 --> 00:03:21.896
Z keeps the two where the bits
agree, zero zero and one one.

00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:26.009
The question X X swaps those two
with each other, so answering

00:03:26.009 --> 00:03:29.261
plus one to it means carrying
the same amplitude on both.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.099
<c.asks>So that leaves one state.
Isn't that the Bell state?</c>

00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:37.962
Yes, it leaves exactly one state, the
Bell state from lecture two, and look at

00:03:37.962 --> 00:03:42.256
what it took to pin it down, two short
strings instead of four amplitudes.

00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:45.938
The set of states that a list
fixes like this is called the

00:03:45.938 --> 00:03:49.311
space stabilized by the list, and
the list itself, together with

00:03:49.311 --> 00:03:52.363
all the products of its strings,
is called the stabilizer.

00:03:52.933 --> 00:03:55.826
The strings you actually write
down are its generators, and

00:03:55.826 --> 00:03:58.121
those are the only ones you
ever need to check.

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:02.643
Now we can go back to the bit
flip code, and watch its two

00:04:02.643 --> 00:04:05.776
checks do their work on all eight
dimensions of three qubits.

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:10.309
The first check, Z Z on qubits one
and two, answers plus one on

00:04:10.309 --> 00:04:14.633
exactly half of them, so it keeps
this half, and we can dim the rest.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.964
The second check asks about qubits
two and three, and it cuts across

00:04:18.964 --> 00:04:22.166
the first one, so again only half
of what's left survives.

00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:26.589
So with three qubits and two checks, how
many dimensions do you think are left?

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:31.583
<c.asks>Eight, then four, then
two. That's one qubit.</c>

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:36.484
Yes, two dimensions, which is one
qubit's worth of room, and those two

00:04:36.484 --> 00:04:41.400
are exactly zero zero zero and one one
one, the code space we started with.

00:04:41.967 --> 00:04:46.032
And the same thing happens for any
number of qubits. You start with two

00:04:46.032 --> 00:04:49.980
to the n dimensions, every new check
halves that, and after n minus k

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.696
checks you're left with two to the
k, which is room for k qubits.

00:04:54.067 --> 00:04:58.281
<c.asks>Any check at all? What if I
add Z on one and Z on three?</c>

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:02.517
Good, Z on one and three is a nice
one to try, because it's just the

00:05:02.517 --> 00:05:06.504
product of the two checks we already
have, so this cut removes nothing.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.269
That's why the count only
works for independent

00:05:09.269 --> 00:05:11.767
checks, where none of them
is a product of the others.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.569
So for fifty qubits, the list is at
most fifty strings of fifty

00:05:17.569 --> 00:05:21.298
letters, which is about five
thousand bits, while the amplitudes

00:05:21.298 --> 00:05:25.204
come to two to the fifty complex
numbers, around a million billion.

00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:28.502
<c.asks>So can every state be
written as a list like this?</c>

00:05:28.933 --> 00:05:32.308
No, not every state, and I should
be honest about that. Only very

00:05:32.308 --> 00:05:35.735
special states can be written as
a list like this, but they happen

00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:38.846
to include every code we'll meet
in the rest of the lecture.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:43.212
So a code doesn't have to be
handed to you as a table of

00:05:43.212 --> 00:05:46.471
amplitudes. You can hand over a
short list of questions instead.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.179
<c.asks>And the three boxes we dimmed,
are those just thrown away?</c>

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.615
They're not thrown away at all,
and I think that's the best part.

00:05:54.615 --> 00:05:57.754
Those three boxes are where a
single bit flip sends the code,

00:05:57.754 --> 00:06:00.841
and which box you land in tells
you which qubit was flipped.

00:06:01.433 --> 00:06:04.780
That's what we'll take up next,
in section eight point two, where

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.866
the answers that come back minus
one turn into the syndrome.
